{"id": "q0111", "pool": "frozen", "arm": "balanced", "tier": "balanced", "lang": "en", "run_id": "ttfa1", "attempt": 1, "seed": 3298877096, "protocol": "ttfa-v3", "config_sha256": "0c0eb5f5089b4570f4b5f593318ef7c0c5f8ba06a58bdaf93a6686b9afa1d86e", "gate_art_sha256": "0e6494c2eeac9bcd86c10b5def3cbd32e98bb0765fa2fd8afc8c1b47915ea372", "threshold": 0.7972545623779297, "gpu": "NVIDIA H100 80GB HBM3", "host": "modal", "shard": 1, "query": "Find all the integer roots of\n\\[x^4 + 5x^3 + 9x^2 - x - 14 = 0.\\]Enter all the integer roots, separated by commas.\n\nSolve and give the final answer.", "input_path": "/data/audio_pool/q0111.wav", "input_sha256": "403ff1eba2578b65b01ae44c3e883f600df61b1a425fdbbbdbdb013e4eb5f218", "input_samples": 168600, "input_s": 10.537, "n_real_chunks": 11, "score": 0.7553, "act": 0.9986, "is_info": true, "fired": false, "mode": "local", "answer": "Okay, so we have this equation x cubed minus 4x square d plus 5x minus 14 equal s zero. And I' m looking for the integer roots of that. So one way to approach this is by trying possible factors or values that could make the whole thing zero. Since it's a cubic, there might be up to three real root s, but we're only interested in the integer ones. A good starting point is to use the rational root theorem, which says that any potential rational root p over q, where p is a factor of the constant term and q is a factor of the leading coefficient, could work here. The constant term is negative fourteen, so its factors are one, two, seven, fourteen, and their negatives. The leading coefficient is one, so the only possible value for q is one. That means our possible integer roots are just the factor s of negative fourteen: negative fourteen, negative seven, negative two, negative one, one, two, seven, and fourteen. Let me try plugging these into the equation one at a time. Starting with x equals one. If I substitute one in, I get one cub ed minus four times one squared plus five times one minus fourteen. One minus four is negative three, plus five gives two, two minus fourteen is negative twelve. Not zero, so one isn't a root. How about x equals two? Two cube d is eight, minus four times four is sixteen, so eight minus sixteen is negative eight, plus ten gives two, two minus fourteen is still negative twelve. Nope, not that either. Try x equals seven. Seven cubed is 34 3, minus four times forty- nine is 19 6, so 3 43 minus 1 96 is about 1", "relay_text": "", "expert_answer": "", "transcript": "", "asr_s": null, "expert_s": null, "expert_input_samples": null, "expert_input_s": null, "expert_input_sha256": null, "expert_timed_out": false, "wait_s": null, "relay_synth_ms": null, "onset_chunk": 10, "eot_seen": false, "n_speak_chunks": 60, "feed_lag_max_ms": 21305, "feed_lag_sum_ms": 568394, "audio_files": {"local": {"path": "/data/ttfa_real/ttfa1/frozen/audio/q0111_balanced_local.wav", "sha256": "51aa8ec0067004ce204b2de42fadff964a59532da61b30391394685ca2b39a46", "sr": 24000, "seconds": 60.0}}, "chunks": [[0, 1, 1.0, 1.116, 1.212, 1, 0, 0], [1, 1, 2.0, 2.121, 2.218, 1, 0, 0], [2, 1, 3.0, 3.121, 3.216, 1, 0, 0], [3, 1, 4.0, 4.12, 4.217, 1, 0, 0], [4, 1, 5.0, 5.115, 5.21, 1, 0, 0], [5, 1, 6.0, 6.115, 6.211, 1, 0, 0], [6, 1, 7.0, 7.122, 7.221, 1, 0, 0], [7, 1, 8.0, 8.123, 8.223, 1, 0, 0], [8, 1, 9.0, 9.123, 9.221, 1, 0, 0], [9, 1, 10.0, 10.12, 10.22, 1, 0, 0], [10, 1, 10.537, 10.659, 11.568, 0, 24000, 0], [11, 0, 11.537, 11.686, 12.737, 0, 24000, 0], [12, 0, 12.537, 12.856, 13.934, 0, 24000, 0], [13, 0, 13.537, 14.053, 15.21, 0, 24000, 0], [14, 0, 14.537, 15.328, 16.547, 0, 24000, 0], [15, 0, 15.537, 16.666, 17.789, 0, 24000, 0], [16, 0, 16.538, 17.911, 19.139, 0, 24000, 0], [17, 0, 17.538, 19.26, 20.382, 0, 24000, 0], [18, 0, 18.538, 20.503, 21.694, 0, 24000, 0], [19, 0, 19.538, 21.821, 23.078, 0, 24000, 0], [20, 0, 20.538, 23.202, 24.363, 0, 24000, 0], [21, 0, 21.538, 24.489, 25.666, 0, 24000, 0], [22, 0, 22.538, 25.789, 27.104, 0, 24000, 0], [23, 0, 23.538, 27.23, 28.201, 0,